.TH SECSTORE 2
.SH NAME
secstore \- fetch data from Plan 9's secure storage service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "dial.m";
include "secstore.m";
secstore := load Secstore Secstore->PATH;

Maxfilesize: con 128*1024;	# default

init:       fn();
privacy:    fn(): int;
cansecstore:    fn(addr: string, user: string): int;
mkseckey:   fn(pass: string): array of byte;
mkseckey2:  fn(pass: string): array of byte;
mkseckey4:  fn(rootkey: array of byte): array of byte;
connect4:   fn(addr: string, user: string,
                pwhash, pwhash2, pwhash4: array of byte):
                (ref Dial->Connection, string, string);
setverifier: fn(conn: ref Dial->Connection, version, hexHi: string): string;
vaultkey:   fn(dk: array of byte): array of byte;
vaultstate: fn(conn: ref Dial->Connection): (int, string);
showvault:  fn(conn: ref Dial->Connection, wk: array of byte): (int, string);
enrolvault: fn(conn: ref Dial->Connection, wk: array of byte): string;
unenrolvault: fn(conn: ref Dial->Connection): string;
dial:       fn(addr: string): ref Dial->Connection;
auth:       fn(conn: ref Dial->Connection, user: string, seckey: array of byte):
                (string, string);
connect:    fn(addr: string, user: string, seckey: array of byte):
                (ref Dial->Connection, string, string);
sendpin:    fn(conn: ref Dial->Connection, pin: string): int;
files:       fn(conn: ref Dial->Connection):
                (list of (string, int, string, string, array of byte), string);
getfile:    fn(conn: ref Dial->Connection, name: string,
                maxsize: int): array of byte;
fetch:      fn(conn: ref Dial->Connection, name: string,
                maxsize: int): (array of byte, int, string);
putfile:    fn(conn: ref Dial->Connection, name: string, data: array of byte): int;
remove:     fn(conn: ref Dial->Connection, file: string): int;
bye:        fn(conn: ref Dial->Connection);

mkfilekey3: fn(user, pass: string): array of byte;
encrypt3:   fn(data: array of byte, rootkey: array of byte): array of byte;
decrypt3:   fn(data: array of byte, rootkey: array of byte,
                gcm1key: array of byte, legacykey: array of byte): array of byte;
iscurrent:  fn(data: array of byte): int;
mkfilekey2: fn(pass: string): array of byte;
mkfilekey:  fn(pass: string): array of byte;
erasekey:   fn(key: array of byte);

lines:      fn(file: array of byte): list of array of byte;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Secstore
establishes a secure authenticated connection with a Plan 9
.I secstore
service (or equivalent, such as Plan 9 from User Space),
that can then be used to fetch and decrypt data files, such as the
.B factotum
file containing the initial keys for an instance of
.IR factotum (4).
The module's functions hold the file descriptors for the connection in a
.BR Dial->Connection
value,
as returned by
.IR dial (2).
The
.I addr
parameter that gives the network address of the
.I secstore
service is also as defined in
.IR dial (2).
A nil value defaults to
.BR "net!$auth!secstore" ,
for translation in the usual way by
.IR cs (8).
.PP
.B Init
must be called before invoking any other operation of the module.
.PP
.B Privacy
ensures the memory of the calling process cannot be read, for instance by
.IR prog (3).
It returns zero on success and a negative value on failure.
.PP
.B Cansecstore
returns true if a connection can be made to a
.I secstore
at network address
.IR addr ,
and the given
.I user
has a
.I secstore
account;
it returns false otherwise.
.PP
Users authenticate themselves to the service using a secret key and a special protocol that does not
reveal the key itself to the remote service.
The textual secret (eg, password or pass phrase) is not used directly by the following functions,
but only after transformation by
.BR mkseckey ,
which hashes it into an array of bytes.
That is the
.I key
parameter to the functions.
.PP
.B Dial
dials the
.I secstore
at network address
.I addr
(as defined by
.IR dial (2))
and returns a reference to the resulting
.BR Dial->Connection .
It returns nil on an error and sets the error string.
.PP
.B Auth
authenticates a fresh connection as belonging to a given
.I user
of the service.
The parameter
.I conn
refers to the
.B Dial->Connection
value representing the connection.
.I User
names a user registered with the service.
The parameter
.I seckey
is the result of applying
.B mkseckey
to the user's secret.
.I Auth
returns a tuple
.BI ( srvname,\ diag ).
.I Srvname
is the service name configured in the remote host (often simply
.BR secstore ).
On an error,
.I srvname
is nil, and
.I diag
is a diagnostic.
If the remote service
has been configured to demand extra authentication data, then
.I diag
contains a demand for it.
Currently the only such value is
.RB ` need pin ';
call
.B sendpin
to provide it to the connection.
If
.B sendpin
succeeds, it returns zero, and
.I conn
can be used normally; on error,
.B sendpin
returns -1 and the connection cannot be used.
.PP
.B Connect
combines the actions of
.B dial
and
.BR auth :
dials the
.I secstore
at
.IR addr ,
and mutually authenticates the server and the given
.I user
using the user's secret
.I key
for that service.
It returns a tuple
.BI ( conn,\ srvname,\ diag ),
where each component is as described for
.B dial
and
.B auth
above.
On an error,
.I conn
is nil, and
.I diag
contains a diagnostic.
.PP
.B Getfile
retrieves the file
.I name
from the secure store, and returns its contents as an array of bytes.
.I Maxsize
gives the largest acceptable file size; if the value is zero or negative,
a large value is used by default.
It returns nil, with the error string set, both when the server has no such file
and when the file could not be read.
.B Fetch
tells them apart: it returns a tuple
.BI ( file,\ absent,\ err ),
the file's contents with
.I absent
zero and
.I err
nil; or nil with
.I absent
non-zero when the server says it has no file of that name; or nil and a diagnostic
when the file could not be read (the server's own error, an answer that is not a size,
a connection that ended part way).
A caller that will later store the file should use
.BR fetch :
a file that could not be read is not a file that is not there.
.B Files
returns the account's files as a list of tuples
.BI ( name,\ size,\ date,\ hash,\ rawhash )
and a diagnostic, nil if the listing was read.
A Plan 9
.I secstore
lists each file's SHA-1 hash;
.IR secstored (8)
its SHA-256.
Nothing a server sends raises an exception in these functions: an answer that is not
what the protocol has is an error.
.PP
.B Connect4
is how a client of
.IR secstored (8)
logs in.
.I Pwhash4
is
.B mkseckey4
of the file key's root,
.BI mkfilekey3( user,\ pass ) :
version 4 of the exchange, whose verifier costs as much to guess against as the files do.
.I Pwhash
.RB ( mkseckey )
and
.I pwhash2
.RB ( mkseckey2 )
are for an account not yet at version 4:
it is logged in to at its own version, given its version 4 verifier
.RB ( setverifier ),
and logged in to again, so that the connection returned is a version 4 session.
In such a session
.B putfile
and
.B remove
are answered by the server and return its refusal;
in a session of an older version (from
.BR connect ,
or from a server that knows no version 4) nothing is said either way, and
.IR secstored (8)
stores nothing.
.B Setverifier
replaces the account's verifier, with one made by
.BI mkverifier( user ,\ \&"secstore4" ,\ pwhash4 )
for a new password.
.PP
The files stored on the service are separately encrypted by the client.
.B Encrypt3
encrypts
.I data
under
.I rootkey
(for an ordinary account,
.BR mkfilekey3 's
result; for an account with a second factor, its data key)
in the format called SGCM2:
AES-256-GCM under a key made from
.I rootkey
and a salt of the file's own, the format's name and the salt under the tag.
It returns nil if no random numbers can be had.
.B Decrypt3
decrypts such a file, and returns nil, with the error string set,
if the file is not one or
.I rootkey
is not its key.
It is the only format written.
The two before it (SGCM1; and Plan 9's AES-CBC under a SHA-1 of the password, which has no integrity)
are read by
.B decrypt3
only when it is given their keys,
.I gcm1key
.RB ( mkfilekey2 )
and
.I legacykey
.RB ( mkfilekey ):
a caller that passes nil for both, as a login does, takes SGCM2 and nothing else,
and one that passes them is moving an old file
.RB ( "auth/secstore m" ,
see
.IR secstore (1)).
(They were passed by every caller, so the file chose its own format,
and whoever could put a file in the store could put an old one there.)
.B Iscurrent
says whether
.I data
is in the current format.
.PP
.B Putfile
writes
.I data
under file
.I name
to the secure store, overwriting a possibly existing file by that name.
.I Data
should already be encrypted, by
.BR encrypt3 .
.B Putfile
returns 0 on success and a negative value on error.
.PP
.B Remove
deletes the given
.I file
from the server.
It returns 0 on success and a negative value on error.
.PP
The remaining functions are the second factor's lock on an account's files (see
.IR secstored (8)).
.B Vaultkey
makes the vault key from the data key
.IR dk .
.B Enrolvault
has the server keep its hash: from then on the account's files are stored and removed
only in a session to which
.B showvault
has shown the key.
.B Showvault
returns 1 if the key was taken, 0 if no second factor is enrolled, and -1 with a diagnostic otherwise;
.B vaultstate
returns 1 if one is enrolled, 0 if not.
.B Unenrolvault
removes the enrolment, in a session that has shown the key.
.PP
.B Erasekey
clears the bytes of
.I key
to zero; it should be called on every value produced by the
.B mkfilekey
and
.B mkseckey
functions, after use,
but can also be used on the data arrays returned by
.B getfile
and
.BR decrypt3 .
.PP
.B Lines
returns a list of slices of
.IR file ,
representing each line of
.I file
in turn (including newline).
.IR Factotum (4)
for instance requires keys to be written to its control file one at a time.
.PP
.B Bye
closes the connection to the
.IR secstore .
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/secstore.b
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
As well as returning the error values described above, functions set the system error string.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR crypt (1),
.IR secstore (1),
.IR factotum (2),
.IR factotum (4)
